"Everything in the heavens and on the earth glorifies Allah. This is a reality inherent in the creation of every existent being. Human beings, too, by using their freedom of will, must declare Allah free from every flaw and deficiency, and refrain from associating partners with Him. As stated at the end of the previous surah, they should not be deceived in moments of victory and deviate into excess, but knowing that victory comes from Allah, they should continue to glorify and exalt Him. Allah is al-Quddus, that is, entirely pure and far removed from every imperfection. He is in need of nothing. The mention of glorification (tasbih) and sanctification (taqdis) together at the beginning of this surah indicates the rejection of excesses such as Christians falling into the Trinity following victory. Allah is also the Almighty (al-Aziz, the Exalted and Prevailing) and the All-Wise (al-Hakim, the Possessor of wisdom).\n\nAllah has sent from among the unlettered a messenger from their own midst. The word 'ummi' means one who cannot read or write, and here it refers to the Arabs. This messenger recites Allah's verses to them, purifies them (cleansing them of evil beliefs and traits), and teaches them the Book and wisdom. Whereas previously, they were in clear misguidance. This messenger has been sent not only to the Arabs, but also to all other people who have not yet joined them.\n\nThe sending of this messenger is a grace of Allah; it cannot be acquired through labor. Allah gives it to whomever He wills. Following this, those who were given the Torah but did not act in accordance with it are rebuked. Their condition resembles that of a donkey carrying books on its back without being aware of what they contain. Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people who deny His verses.\n\nAddressing the Jews, it is said: 'If you claim that you are the allies of Allah, then wish for death!' But on account of the sins their hands have put forth, they will never wish for death. Yet death will inevitably come and return them to the presence of Allah, the Knower of the unseen and the witnessed. At that time, everything they did will be made known to them, and they will receive their recompense.\n\nO you who believe! When the call to prayer is made on Friday, hasten to the remembrance of Allah (the sermon and the prayer) and leave off trading. That is better for you. Once the prayer is completed, disperse throughout the land and seek your share of Allah's bounty. And remember Allah much so that you may attain salvation. Regarding the 'call' mentioned here, the view preferred by the Hanafis is that the obligation begins with the first external adhan. As for 'hastening' (sa'y), it does not mean running in a panic, but rather proceeding promptly and striving with intention.\n\nOne of the conditions of the Friday prayer is the sermon (khutbah). The sermon falls under the scope of 'the remembrance of Allah' (dhikrullah) and is delivered before the prayer. According to the Great Imam (Abu Hanifah), the obligatory component of the sermon is the shortest utterance that can be called dhikrullah (such as praising Allah [hamd], declaring His oneness [tahlil], or glorifying Him [tasbih]). Preaching, admonition, and supplications are Sunnah. Among the other Sunnah acts of the sermon are ritual purity, delivering it standing, facing the congregation, commencing with praise, and reciting from the Quran.\n\nWhen the believers saw some merchandise or amusement, they dispersed immediately toward it and left you (the Messenger of Allah) standing. Say: 'That which is with Allah is better than amusement and merchandise. And Allah is the best of providers.' This verse emphasizes that worldly gains hold no value alongside the blessings of the Hereafter.\n\nDuring the sermon, it is Sunnah for the preacher (khatib) to recite three short verses or one long verse from the Quran, to offer praise and glorification to Allah, to invoke blessings upon the Prophet, and to supplicate for believing men and women. It is necessary to sit between the two sermons for the duration of reciting three verses; if this sitting is omitted, the preacher incurs sin. That the sermon be short and the prayer long is in accord with jurisprudence (fiqh) and the Sunnah. It is recommended (mustahabb) for the preacher to raise his voice, and it is recommended to soften it in the second sermon compared to the first. Mentioning the four caliphs, Hamzah, and Abbas in the sermon is regarded as commendable. It is an essential condition that the preacher be qualified to lead the prayer.\n\nWhen the imam ascends the pulpit, it is not permissible to speak, eat, or drink; the congregation must also remain silent. Listening to the sermon is obligatory (wajib), and being close is more virtuous, but stepping over people's shoulders to move forward is disliked (makruh). The essential substance of the sermon is dhikrullah; the prayer is likewise a form of remembrance. In accordance with the verse, 'O you who believe, when the call to prayer is made on Friday, hasten to the remembrance of Allah and leave off trading,' engaging in commerce once the call to prayer is made is forbidden (haram). A sale concluded at the time of the call is not invalid (batil) provided there is no defect in the item sold or the contract itself, but it is prohibitively disliked (tahriman makruh); because the prohibition is not due to the transaction in its essence, but due to the proximity of the time.\n\nFriday is the weekly festival (eid) of Muslims and the master of days. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'Every Friday, Allah frees six hundred thousand people from the Fire.' Whoever dies on Friday is granted the reward of a martyr. Furthermore, there is an hour on that day (the hour of response, sa'at al-ijabah) such that if a Muslim coincides with it while supplicating, Allah grants what he asks. There are various narrations regarding which hour this is; the strongest is that it spans from the moment the imam ascends the pulpit until the prayer concludes. On Friday, the angels stand at the door of the mosque, recording those who arrive early; the one who comes earliest receives a reward as though having offered a camel, then an ox, then a ram, then a chicken, then an egg in charity.\n\nThe first Friday prayer in Medina was performed before the Migration (Hijrah) by As'ad ibn Zurarah with forty men. Mus'ab ibn Umayr, on the other hand, led twelve people in the Friday prayer by order of the Prophet. When the narrations are reconciled, it is understood that As'ad gathered the congregation while Mus'ab served as the imam. In the hadith of Jabir regarding the obligation of the Friday prayer, the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: 'Allah has made Friday [prayer] an obligation upon you in this year, in this month, on this day, and in this place until the Day of Resurrection. Whoever abandons it out of disregard, may Allah not bless his affairs.'\n\nWhen the Prophet (peace be upon him) migrated to Medina, he stayed in Quba for several days and then set out on a Friday. When the time for the Friday prayer entered in a valley belonging to the Banu Salim ibn Awf, he delivered a sermon and led the first Friday prayer. Later in Medina, he ordered a pulpit (minbar) to be constructed; it is narrated that he prayed upon the pulpit built by the carpenter slave of a woman and demonstrated prayer upon the pulpit to serve as an example for the people. The introduction of the pulpit ensured that the sermon was conducted in a more orderly manner.\n\nAccording to the narration of Jabir ibn Abdillah, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) initially used to deliver the sermon leaning against a palm trunk. When the pulpit was built, that trunk began to moan like a she-camel separated from her calf. The Messenger of Allah stepped down from the pulpit and placed his hand upon it, whereupon it fell silent. This event is known as the miracle of 'the moaning of the palm trunk' (Hanin al-Jidh'). The first Friday prayer outside Medina was performed at the mosque of Abd al-Qays in Jawatha, Bahrain.\n\nThe conditions of the Friday prayer are divided into two categories: conditions of obligation (wujub) and conditions of performance (ada'). The conditions of obligation are: freedom, being male, residency (not being a traveler), good health, having the ability to walk, and safety (the absence of blindness, fear of an enemy, torrential rain, and similar conditions). For whoever lacks these conditions, Friday prayer is not obligatory, but if they perform it, their prayer is valid.\n\nThe first of the conditions of performance is the 'misr al-jami',' that is, a major city possessing administrative institutions. According to the Hanafis, this is a place where there is a judge to enforce legal rulings and an authority to ensure security. Imam al-Shafi'i, however, did not stipulate this as a condition, stating that the Friday prayer may be performed anywhere forty free resident men reside. In addition, it is a condition that the Friday prayer be performed within its proper time (the time of Dhuhr); if its time expires, it is not made up as Friday prayer, but rather the Dhuhr prayer is performed.\n\nAnother condition of valid performance is that the head of state (the imam) or a person authorized by him leads the prayer. This is called \"izn-i sultan\" (permission of the ruler). As for the condition of the congregation, it is at least three men along with the imam; Abu Yusuf considers three people including the imam sufficient. The Great Imam Abu Hanifa, however, stipulates four people along with the imam. Although there are many opinions regarding the size of the congregation, the most virtuous is to pray in a crowded mosque.\n\nPerforming ghusl (ritual bath) on Friday is commanded in many hadiths. Hanafi scholars state that this ghusl is sunnah or recommended (mustahabb). Ghusl should be performed after dawn and before going to Friday prayer; additionally, wearing pleasant perfume, using a miswak, and putting on one's finest clothes are recommended. In hadiths narrated from Abdullah ibn Salam and Abu Dharr, it is reported that whoever performs this purification and goes to Friday prayer will have their sins forgiven between the two Fridays.\n\nOne Friday, while the Messenger of Allah was delivering the sermon (khutbah), a trade caravan arrived, and upon hearing the sound of drums, most of the congregation left the mosque and headed toward the caravan. Only twelve people remained with the Messenger of Allah. Thereupon, Allah the Exalted revealed the ayah, \"When they saw a transaction or amusement, they left you standing.\" In the ayah, the believers are reminded that the reward with Allah is better than trade and amusement."